CH = dict(num=17, tag="Political Science · Ch 5", theme="How to judge democracy: accountability, growth, inequality, diversity and dignity",
 title="Outcomes of Democracy",
 intro="Democracy is widely admired but also criticised. To judge it fairly we must ask what outcomes we can reasonably expect: a responsive, accountable government, economic growth and equality, accommodation of diversity, and the dignity and freedom of citizens. This chapter examines each expectation and the evidence from India and elsewhere.",
 big_questions=["How do we assess the outcomes of democracy?", "Does democracy ensure accountable and legitimate government?", "What is its record on growth, inequality and poverty?", "How does it handle diversity and protect dignity and freedom?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "How Do We Assess Democracy's Outcomes?"),
 ("p", "People expect a great deal from democracy, so they are disappointed by it. Democracy is better than other forms because it promotes **equality among citizens, enhances the dignity of the individual, improves the quality of decision making, and provides a method to resolve conflicts**. It also allows a mistake to be corrected. Outcomes should be judged against realistic expectations: democracy is a system of rules and methods, and what it delivers depends also on the citizens and the economy."),
 ("table", ["Area", "What we expect", "Reality"], [["Accountable, responsive and legitimate government", "Government that follows rules, is answerable to citizens, and has public support", "Democracy should produce a government that is **accountable** and **responsive**; the record is mixed (better on free elections and open debate, weak on corruption and sharing information), but democratic government is certainly **legitimate**, as it is the people's own government"], ["Economic growth and development", "Higher growth", "Between **1950 and 2000**, dictatorships had an average growth rate of about **4.42 per cent**, democracies **3.95 per cent**; the gap is small, and among poor countries it is almost nil (4.34 under dictatorship, 4.28 under democracy); growth depends on population size, global situation, cooperation from other countries and economic priorities"], ["Reduction of inequality and poverty", "Equality", "Democracy is based on **political equality** (one person, one vote) but there is growing **economic inequality**; a few ultra-rich hold a large share of income while the poor struggle to meet basic needs; democracy has not reduced economic inequality much"], ["Accommodation of social diversity", "Peaceful handling of differences", "Democracy usually develops procedures to conduct competition peacefully, as in Belgium; but it must not become rule by the majority community, and every citizen must have a chance to be in the majority at some time; Sri Lanka failed on this"], ["Dignity and freedom", "Respect for all, especially the weak", "Democracy has strengthened the dignity of the individual; the dignity of women and the struggle against caste inequality shows the improvement"]]),
 ("h2", "Accountable, Responsive and Legitimate Government"),
 ("ul", ["Citizens have a right to examine the process of decision making, which is called **transparency**. A democratic government develops mechanisms for citizens to hold the government accountable and to participate when needed.", "The **Right to Information (RTI) Act** (2005) lets citizens ask government offices for information; in the textbook example, Nannu, a daily wage earner, used it to get his lost ration card replaced within a week after months of being ignored; this makes governments more accountable. Democracies, however, are weak at sharing information with citizens.", "Democracy may be slow, because decisions are taken after consultation; yet decisions are **more likely to be accepted** and carry **legitimacy**. The government is responsible for any mistakes, which can be reversed in the next election."]),
 ("h2", "Economic Growth, Inequality and Poverty"),
 ("ul", ["Between 1950 and 2000 dictatorships had a slightly higher average growth rate; democracy cannot be said to guarantee development, but it should not lag behind dictatorships, and it has other positive outcomes.", "**Inequality of income:** In democracies, the people at the top control a large share of national income (e.g. in South Africa and Brazil the top 20 per cent take over 60 per cent of national income, leaving less than 3 per cent for the bottom 20 per cent); in Denmark and Hungary the gap is far smaller.", "**Poverty:** The poor are a large part of the voters and no party wants to lose their votes; yet elected governments do not seem as keen to address poverty as expected. In Bangladesh more than half the population lives in poverty, and some poor countries depend on rich countries even for food."]),
 ("h2", "Accommodation of Social Diversity"),
 ("ul", ["Democracies usually develop a procedure to conduct their competition so that social divisions do not become explosive. **No society can fully and permanently resolve conflicts among different groups**, but we can learn to respect these differences and develop mechanisms to negotiate them.", "Democracy is best suited to this outcome, but only on two conditions: democracy is **not simply rule by majority opinion** (the majority must work with the minority), and majority rule must **not become rule by the majority community** of religion, race or language (Sri Lanka). The Belgian model shows accommodation."]),
 ("h2", "Dignity and Freedom of the Citizens"),
 ("ul", ["Democracy stands much superior to any other form of government in promoting dignity and freedom of the individual. Every individual wants to receive respect from fellow beings, and conflicts arise when some are denied dignity.", "**Dignity of women:** long struggles by women have made respect and equal treatment accepted as necessary in a democratic society; women are not always treated with respect in practice, but once the principle is recognised it is easier to struggle against what is now legally and morally unacceptable.", "Challenges: **caste-based inequality and untouchability** continue; but the democratic principle gives the dominated groups a strong moral and legal ground to challenge it."]),
 ("h2", "Making Democracy Work"),
 ("ul", ["Democracy's outcomes are not guaranteed: public participation, trust in institutions, a free press and civic awareness decide how well it works. People's own dissatisfaction is a sign of strength: it shows they are aware and can demand more from the system.", "Therefore the quality of democracy depends on **how we use it**; the fact that people complain and expect more shows democracy's success: it turns a subject into a citizen."]),
 ("h2", "Quick Revision: Likely Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
  ("Why is democracy legitimate?", "It is the people's own government, with their participation and consent."),
  ("What is transparency?", "Citizens' right to examine how decisions are made."),
  ("What does RTI Act 2005 do?", "Gives citizens the right to seek information from government."),
  ("Does democracy ensure higher growth than dictatorship?", "No; 1950-2000 average was 4.42% for dictatorships and 3.95% for democracies, and among poor countries 4.34% and 4.28%."),
  ("Which two conditions must a democracy fulfil to accommodate diversity?", "It is not simply rule by majority opinion, and majority rule must not become rule by the majority community."),
  ("How does democracy handle diversity?", "Through negotiation and accommodation, as Belgium shows."),
  ("Why is democracy better for dignity?", "It treats all citizens as equal and empowers the weak to claim rights."),
 ]),
 ],
 takeaways=["Democracy should be judged by realistic outcomes: **accountability, legitimacy, growth, equality, diversity and dignity**.", "It produces **accountable and responsive** government, strengthened by the **RTI Act (2005)**.", "Growth rates of democracies and dictatorships are close; democracies still struggle with **inequality and poverty**.", "Democracy **accommodates diversity** through negotiation and enhances **dignity and freedom**.", "People's dissatisfaction shows democracy is working; citizens must keep making it better."])
